# Demonstrates the unavoidable lexical scope collision limitation in Otter 1.0.1.
# Under the frozen 1.0.1 specification, all variable assignments inside a function
# search the parent scope chain (D10 / OtterEnvironment.Set).
# If a variable name exists in any enclosing scope, the function's assignment
# silently mutates the caller's variable or crashes if an object definition is used.

# 1. Unavoidable Object-Definition Collision
# If a caller variable happens to match the internal name used for object instantiation:
a Box has
    val
.

to internalObjectFactory n
    # Even if this variable name is prefixed or namespaced, if a caller
    # shares the name, this statement causes a fatal runtime crash:
    _factory_box is a Box
    val of _factory_box is n
    return _factory_box
.

# Caller has a variable named _factory_box:
_factory_box is "caller_vital_data"

# This call crashes with exit code 3:
# "Otter will not replace existing string called "_factory_box" with a new thing."
b is internalObjectFactory 42
say "This line is unreachable under Otter 1.0.1 semantics"
